\n   152\t\n   153\t## Dependency Review Expectations\n   154\t\n   155\tYalla p
ulls only what it needs. When you add or update a third-party\n   156\tGo module
, follow the checklist in\n   157\t[`CONTRIBUTING.md`](./CONTRIBUTING.md#depende
ncy-review-checklist) and\n   158\texpect the dependency-review CI job to gate t
he PR. New modules with\n   159\tunknown licenses or known high-severity advisor
ies will be rejected\n   160\tautomatically.\n   161\t\n   162\t## Container Ima
ge Hardening\n   163\t\n   164\tThe production `Dockerfile` ships the `yalla-api
` binary and\n   165\t`Dockerfile.worker` ships the `yalla-worker` binary inside
a\n   166\t`gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot` runtime so a leaked or\n 
 167\tcompromised image carries no shell, no package manager, no busybox,\n   16
8\tand no setuid binaries — only the service binary and the system CA\n   169\tb
undle. The hardening posture is part of the public contract and is\n   170\tpinn
ed by `internal/release/container_hardening_static_test.go`; a\n   171\tregressi
on in any one of the following is caught at build time:\n   172\t\n   173\t- **M
ulti-stage build.** The Go toolchain, module cache, and source\n   174\t  tree n
ever reach the runtime layer. A single-stage Dockerfile is\n   175\t  rejected.\
n   176\t- **Distroless `nonroot` runtime.** The runtime stage\'s base image\n  
177\t  is pinned to `gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot` (UID/GID\n   178
\t  65532). Debian/Ubuntu/Alpine slim runtimes are rejected because\n   179\t  t
hey reintroduce a shell and a package manager.\n   180\t- **`USER nonroot:nonroo
t`.** The runtime stage MUST run as the\n   181\t  non-root user. A missing or p
artial (`USER nonroot`) directive is\n   182\t  rejected.\n   183\t- **Hardened 
build flags.** The builder stage MUST invoke\n   184\t  `go build` with `CGO_ENA
BLED=0` (static binary compatible with\n   185\t  the distroless static image), 
`-trimpath` (strip local filesystem\n   186\t  paths from the binary), and `-ldf
lags="-s -w"` (drop the symbol\n   187\t  and DWARF tables). Each missing flag i
s a separate regression.\n   188\t- **No `ADD <url>`.** Fetching arbitrary conte
nt at build time\n   189\t  without checksum verification is forbidden. Use `COP
Y` or a\n   190\t  `RUN curl ... | sha256sum -c` pattern.\n   191\t- **No baked 
secrets.** The Dockerfile MUST NOT declare ENV values\n   192\t  whose key names
contain `TOKEN`, `PASSWORD`, `SECRET`, `API_KEY`,\n   193\t  `PRIVATE_KEY`, `SIG
NING_KEY`, or `DSN`. Secrets are supplied by\n   194\t  the operator at runtime 
through environment variables, never\n   195\t  baked into the image.\n   196\t-
**No package install in the runtime stage.** `apt-get`,\n   197\t  `apt install`
, `yum install`, `dnf install`, `microdnf install`,\n   198\t  and `apk add` are
rejected in the final layer. The builder stage\n   199\t  is free to install bui
ld dependencies; the runtime stage is not.\n   200\t- **`COPY --from=builder --c
hown=nonroot:nonroot`.** Every\n   201\t  cross-stage COPY into the runtime laye
r MUST chown to the\n   202\t  non-root user so the copied artefact is not owned
by root.\n   203\t- **Explicit `ENTRYPOINT`.** The runtime stage MUST declare an
\n   204\t  `ENTRYPOINT` (not just `CMD`) so `docker run -- <args>` cannot\n   2
05\t  replace the binary at launch.\n   206\t- **Worker image contract.** `Docke
rfile.worker` MUST build\n   207\t  `./cmd/yalla-worker`, copy `/out/yalla-worke
r` to\n   208\t  `/usr/local/bin/yalla-worker`, and use that binary as the\n   2
09\t  entrypoint. The worker has no HTTP listener, so orchestrators\n   210\t  s
hould use process liveness, non-zero exit restarts, durable\n   211\t  provision
ing job state, dead-letter alerts, worker queue metrics,\n   212\t  and structur
ed `service=yalla-worker` logs instead of an in-image\n   213\t  HTTP health pro
be.\n   214\t\n   215\tOperators are expected to run the image with `--read-only
`,\n   216\t`--cap-drop=ALL`, and a non-host network. The Dockerfile\'s header\n
  217\tcomment documents the canonical `docker run` invocation; the worker\n   2
18\tDockerfile documents the corresponding no-port runtime contract.\n   219\t\n
  220\tThe local operations stack (`docker-compose.yml`) MUST keep the\n   221\t
Postgres-only integration-test workflow available while also\n   222\tversioning
an opt-in `control-plane` profile for `yalla-api` and\n   223\t`yalla-worker`. T
he Postgres image is pinned to a major version tag\n   224\t(`postgres:16`, not 
`postgres` and not `postgres:latest`) and\n   225\tdeclares a `healthcheck` so t
he integration-test harness has a\n   226\tdeterministic readiness gate. The API
and worker services build from\n   227\tthe production `Dockerfile` / `Dockerfil
e.worker`, wait for\n   228\t`postgres.condition=service_healthy`, run with a re
ad-only root\n   229\tfilesystem, `cap_drop: [ALL]`, `no-new-privileges:true`, a
nd a `/tmp`\n   230\ttmpfs, and use required environment-variable interpolation 
for every\n   231\tsecret-shaped value. `yalla-api` publishes `8080:8080` so ope
rators\n   232\tcan probe `GET /healthz` and `GET /readyz`; `yalla-worker` still
has\n   233\tno HTTP listener and is observed through structured stdout logs plu
s\n   234\tdurable job state.\n   235\t\n   236\t## Kubernetes Operations Artifa
ct\n   237\t\n   238\tThe production Kubernetes baseline lives at\n   239\t`depl
oy/kubernetes/yalla-control-plane.yaml` with the operator runbook in\n   240\t`d
eploy/kubernetes/README.md`. The artifact references the hardened\n   241\t`ghcr
.io/juribadev/yalla-api` and `ghcr.io/juribadev/yalla-worker`\n   242\timages, r
uns both pods as non-root with a read-only root filesystem,\n   243\tdrops all L
inux capabilities, disables service-account token mounting,\n   244\tand uses `R
untimeDefault` seccomp.\n   245\t\n   246\tThe checked-in manifest intentionally
does **not** include a Kubernetes\n   247\t`Secret`. Runtime credentials are bou
nd by `secretKeyRef` from the\n   248\toperator-created `yalla-control-plane-sec
rets` object so database URLs,\n   249\tsigning keys, Dokploy endpoints, and Dok
ploy tokens are never baked into\n   250\timages or repository files.\n   251\t\
n   252\t`yalla-api` exposes `/healthz` and `/readyz` on port 8080 for Kubernete
s\n   253\tliveness and readiness probes. `yalla-worker` has no HTTP listener; i
ts\n   254\thealth model is process liveness, durable job state, worker metrics,
and\n   255\tstructured JSON logs. Operators can render the artifact offline wit
h\n   256\t`kubectl kustomize deploy/kubernetes` or validate it against a cluste
r with\n   257\t`kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f deploy/kubernetes/yalla-contr
ol-plane.yaml`.\n   258\tCI pins the contract with\n   259\t`go test ./internal/
release/... -run TestKubernetesArtifact`.\n   260\t\n   261\t## systemd Operatio
ns Artifact\n   262\t\n   263\tThe single-node systemd baseline lives under `dep
loy/systemd/` with separate\n   264\tunits for `deploy/systemd/yalla-api.service
` and\n   265\t`deploy/systemd/yalla-worker.service`. The API unit executes\n   
266\t`/usr/local/bin/yalla-api`; the worker unit executes\n   267\t`/usr/local/b
in/yalla-worker`. Both run as the dedicated non-root `yalla`\n   268\tservice ac
count, load runtime configuration from\n   269\t`/etc/yalla/control-plane.env`, 
and write structured JSON diagnostics to\n   270\tjournald.\n   271\t\n   272\tT
he checked-in units intentionally do **not** include database URLs, signing\n   
273\tkeys, secret-encryption keys, Dokploy endpoints, or Dokploy tokens. Operato
rs\n   274\tcopy `deploy/systemd/control-plane.env.example` to\n   275\t`/etc/ya
lla/control-plane.env`, replace the `<redacted:...>` placeholders from\n   276\t
their secret manager, and keep the rendered file outside the repository with\n  
277\tmode `0640` and group `yalla`.\n   278\t\n   279\tBoth units preserve a lea
st-privilege sandbox: `NoNewPrivileges=true`,\n   280\t`ProtectSystem=strict`, `
ProtectHome=true`, private temporary and device\n   281\tnamespaces, empty capab
ility sets, native syscall architecture, and address\n   282\tfamilies limited t
o `AF_UNIX`, `AF_INET`, and `AF_INET6`. `yalla-api` binds\n   283\t`127.0.0.1:80
80` by default for a local TLS-terminating reverse proxy and\n   284\texposes `G
ET /healthz` plus `GET /readyz`; `yalla-worker` has no HTTP listener\n   285\tan
d is observed through process liveness, durable job state, worker metrics,\n   2
86\tdead-letter alerts, and structured logs.\n   287\t\n   288\tOperators can dr
y-run the unit syntax with\n   289\t`systemd-analyze verify deploy/systemd/yalla
-api.service deploy/systemd/yalla-worker.service`.\n   290\tCI pins the artifact
with\n   291\t`go test ./internal/release/... -run TestSystemdArtifact`.\n   292
\t\n   293\t## Database Migration Command Artifact\n   294\t\n   295\tThe produc
tion database migration command lives at\n   296\t`deploy/operations/migrate-dat
abase.sh` with the runbook in\n   297\t`deploy/operations/README.md`. It runs th
e API binary\'s embedded migration\n   298\trunner through `/usr/local/bin/yalla
-api --migrate-only`, so the SQL ladder\n   299\tapplied in production is exactl
y the version compiled into the backend\n   300\trelease. The worker binary rema
ins separate: the script stops `yalla-worker`\n   301\tbefore applying schema ch
anges and restarts both `yalla-api` and\n   302\t`yalla-worker` after a successf
ul migration.\n   303\t\n   304\tThe command loads runtime configuration from `/
etc/yalla/control-plane.env`\n   305\tor `YALLA_CONTROL_PLANE_ENV_FILE`. It neve
r checks in or prints database URLs,\n   306\tsigning keys, secret-encryption ke
ys, Dokploy endpoints, Dokploy tokens, API\n   307\tkeys, cookies, or rendered e
nvironment values; `--dry-run` prints only command\n   308\tshape plus redacted 
`YALLA_*` variable names. After `--apply`, operators verify\n   309\tthe API thr
ough `/healthz` and `/readyz` and inspect structured JSON logs with\n   310\t`jo
urnalctl -u yalla-api -u yalla-worker -o json`.\n   311\t\n   312\tCI pins the c
ommand, runbook, binary flag, and release gate with\n   313\t`go test ./internal
/release/... -run TestDatabaseMigrationCommand`.\n   314\t\n   315\t## Database 
Migration Authoring Artifact\n   316\t\n   317\tThe database migration authoring
guide lives at\n   318\t`docs/development/database-migration-authoring.md`. It d
ocuments how to create\n   319\tpaired `NNNN_description.up.sql` and `NNNN_descr
iption.down.sql` files under\n   320\t`internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migra
tions`, how the embedded runner\n   321\trecords versions in `schema_migrations`
, and how developers verify migrations\n   322\tagainst isolated Postgres databa
ses before merge.\n   323\t\n   324\tThe guide keeps runtime secrets operator-ma
naged and uses only\n   325\t`<redacted:...>` placeholders for local environment
values. It documents the\n   326\tcanonical checks: `go test -run TestMigrations
./...`,\n   327\t`go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...`,\n   328\t`go test -
run TestMigrationsDowngradeSafety ./...`, `go test ./...`,\n   329\t`go test -ra
ce ./...`, `go vet ./...`, and `scripts/verify.sh`. It also\n   330\tdocuments f
ailure recovery for unhealthy local Postgres, dirty migration\n   331\tstates, d
owngrade failures, and production rollback through backup or forward\n   332\tfi
xes rather than ad hoc SQL.\n   333\t\n   334\tCI pins the guide and release gat
e with\n   335\t`go test ./internal/release/... -run TestDatabaseMigrationAuthor
ingArtifact`.\n   336\t\n   337\t## Worker Job Authoring Artifact\n   338\t\n   
339\tThe worker job authoring guide lives at\n   340\t`docs/development/worker-j
ob-authoring.md`. It documents how durable\n   341\tprovisioning work flows from
the Yalla API to Postgres source of truth, then\n   342\tthrough `yalla-worker` 
to the private Dokploy API. The guide covers\n   343\t`provisioning_jobs`, `Stor
eClaimer`, `Provisioner`, `JobRunner`,\n   344\t`store.JobRepository.ClaimNext`,
`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`,\n   345\tlease-owner outcome checks, termin
al failure classification, fake Dokploy\n   346\tfixtures, tenant-scoped `dokplo
y_refs`, and redacted job/audit metadata.\n   347\t\n   348\tThe guide keeps run
time secrets operator-managed and uses only\n   349\t`<redacted:...>` placeholde
rs for local environment values. It documents the\n   350\tcanonical checks: `go
test ./internal/controlplane/worker/...`,\n   351\t`go test -run TestJobWorkerLe
ase ./...`,\n   352\t`go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`,\n   353\t`go test ./i
nternal/controlplane/store/...`, `go test ./...`,\n   354\t`go test -race ./...`
, `go vet ./...`, and `scripts/verify.sh`. It also\n   355\tdocuments stable `ya
lla.output.v1` and `yalla.error.v1` envelopes, request IDs,\n   356\tisolated Po
stgres integration tests, fake-Dokploy-by-default behavior,\n   357\tdead-letter
recovery, `E_JOB_NOT_CLAIMED`, `E_JOB_CANCELLED`, and the opt-in\n   358\textern
al live-Dokploy smoke test that must never run against production.\n   359\t\n  
360\tCI pins the guide and release gate with\n   361\t`go test ./internal/releas
e/... -run TestWorkerJobAuthoringArtifact`.\n   362\t\n   363\t## Fake Dokploy U
sage Artifact\n   364\t\n   365\tThe fake Dokploy usage guide lives at\n   366\t
`docs/development/fake-dokploy-usage.md`. It documents how backend tests use\n  
367\t`internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake` and `dokployfake.New` for\n   3
68\tDokploy-shaped behavior without reaching a live Dokploy server. The guide\n 
 369\tcovers the Yalla API -> Postgres source of truth -> provisioning worker ->
\n   370\tprivate Dokploy API boundary, typed client usage in\n   371\t`internal
/controlplane/dokploy`, worker provisioning paths in\n   372\t`internal/controlp
