        output='     1\t# Observability Dashboard Guide\n     2\t\n     3\tThis 
guide documents how to build, validate, and maintain the observability\n     4\t
dashboard for the Yalla Control Plane API and worker. It covers the versioned\n 
   5\t backend binaries `/usr/local/bin/yalla-api` and `/usr/local/bin/yalla-wor
ker`,\n     6\t Customer / Agent / CI traffic, Postgres source of truth, Provisi
oning worker,\n     7\t durable provisioning jobs, PostgreSQL, and the private D
okploy API dependency. Customer traffic must flow through\n     8\t Yalla; Custo
mers must never receive Dokploy API tokens or call Dokploy directly.\n     9\t\n
   10\tThe dashboard aggregates telemetry from HTTP request metrics, trace spans
, quota\n    11\t decisions, policy decisions, audit events, Dokploy dependency 
metrics, worker\n    12\t queue metrics, reconciliation drift alerts, dead-lette
r alerts, SLO burn-rate\n    13\t metrics, secret-redaction canaries, readiness 
degradation metrics, and slow-query\n    14\t metrics. All metrics are emitted a
s structured JSON logs or Prometheus-compatible\n    15\t time-series snapshots 
exposed through `GET /metrics`.\n    16\t\n    17\tRuntime configuration is oper
ator-managed. The deployed API and worker read\n    18\t `/etc/yalla/control-pla
ne.env`; keep that file outside the repository and\n    19\t populate it from th
e approved secret manager. Dashboard config, screenshots, incident notes, ticket
s, audit metadata, dry-run output, and log excerpts must not print database URLs
. Dashboard evidence must never contain tokens, API keys, cookies, database URLs
, Dokploy tokens, or rendered environment variable values. Use redacted placehol
ders such as `<redacted:YALLA_DATABASE_URL>` when the variable name matters.\n  
 20\t\n    21\t## Dashboard Sources\n    22\t\n    23\tThe observability dashboa
rd is composed from the following low-cardinality metric\n    24\t series. Do no
t group by tenant, principal, resource, service, or job IDs.\n    25\t\n    26\t
| Source | Endpoint / Collector | Use |\n    27\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    28\t| 
HTTP request metrics | `telemetry.HTTPRequestMetrics` in `internal/controlplane/
telemetry` | Group by method, normalized route, status code, and status class. |
\n    29\t| Trace spans | `telemetry.TraceSpanMetrics` | Group by name, kind, ro
ute, component, outcome, status class, and error code. |\n    30\t| Dokploy depe
ndencies | `telemetry.DokployDependencyMetrics` | Group by method, normalized en
dpoint, outcome, status class, error code, and retryable. |\n    31\t| Quota usa
ge | `telemetry.QuotaUsageMetrics` | Group by resource, enforcement mode, outcom
e, and reason. |\n    32\t| Policy decisions | `telemetry.PolicyDecisionMetrics`
| Group by action, resource kind, decision, and reason. |\n    33\t| Audit event
s | `telemetry.AuditEventMetrics` | Group by action, resource kind, decision, ou
tcome, reason, and error code. |\n    34\t| Worker queue | Worker queue metrics 
| Group by event, job type, status, and status class. |\n    35\t| Reconciliatio
n drift | `telemetry.ReconciliationDriftAlertMetrics` | Group by drift kind, act
ion type, reason, severity, and status. |\n    36\t| Dead-letter alerts | `telem
etry.DeadLetterAlertMetrics` | Group by job type, reason, severity, and status. 
|\n    37\t| SLO burn-rate | `telemetry.SLOBurnRateMetrics` | Group by objective
, window, severity, status, and signal. |\n    38\t| Secret-redaction canaries |
`telemetry.SecretRedactionCanaryMetrics` | Group by surface, vector, outcome, an
d reason. |\n    39\t| Readiness degradation | `telemetry.ReadinessDegradationMe
trics` | Group by check, status, and reason. |\n    40\t| Slow queries | `teleme
try.SlowQueryMetrics` | Group by operation, query kind, and outcome. |\n    41\t
\n    42\tRequest, correlation, organization, principal, resource, service, and 
job IDs are\n    43\t join hints only. They are stored as latest-sample incident
hints and must never\n    44\t be used as dashboard grouping labels.\n    45\t\n
   46\t## Required Environment Variables\n    47\t\n    48\tThe observability st
ack is configured through the same environment file used by\n    49\t the API an
d worker:\n    50\t\n    51\t```bash\n    52\t# Operator-managed secrets (never 
